#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514546} :activity/id #uuid "a689b044-d854-52e7-a771-5a0c8c6fa555" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048933 :topic/id #uuid "cd37bb30-463c-55ad-8ec5-08aa1d921272" :topic/math-academy-id 4238} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fea291ee-6497-5ad3-94fc-370909961651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378971} {:answer/id #uuid "d36d82c5-f411-510f-b777-9fca82b43277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378972} {:answer/id #uuid "34611fcc-fc75-5837-8815-4b563c9bc527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378973} {:answer/id #uuid "a08c13c3-88ad-550d-9cc8-cabd76a06564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378974} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d304bd-fbab-5bd6-8c82-afcb34c6646e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378971} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fbad7d-97f9-581f-9baa-b0e8b8e58fe8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6d3c8fe2-1d6d-57e9-bf5c-5e70cb30abf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-170561" :question/problem "The minimum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}-36{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $4{x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $-72.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -54 \\\\ -36 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-72,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-72.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-72:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-72)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 36\\end{bmatrix}+72\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}108 & -108 \\\\ -108 & 108\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-72)I)x=0$ gives the  eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7b55ab94-44ca-5e5f-8316-aab35be9951d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1579c753-1597-556d-8bd8-51a8deed3084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410166} {:answer/id #uuid "1c96736a-b709-578c-8d05-cc73305aecdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} {:answer/id #uuid "31146e93-a5b1-5fe1-90a1-7f9c284f1056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410171} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd5c912-e67d-530f-a2c1-baaeaac814c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410177} {:answer/id #uuid "a21806c6-2164-532f-ac84-142d42881117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410168} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc24884-5d5e-5b11-b5e7-3763c0022ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f433543-b9f1-5f20-b0ed-749a73af3fa7" :question/math-academy-id "q-170506" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=4,$ is $20.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 16 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $20,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=20.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-20I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-20\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 16 \\\\ 16 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-20I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get  \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ebe10e4f-7dda-5f32-aa46-5a154ec9adc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a7fdb-eefe-51a3-9ec9-f7c801ef2416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513564} {:answer/id #uuid "7200c83a-2142-5d00-85db-a56671970e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513836} {:answer/id #uuid "7223184f-b1ee-5d86-a4ef-23d7fc3ee742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513837} {:answer/id #uuid "924f6560-699b-5491-8855-7e4d83c4748a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513912} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6ff50e-3a08-5efc-b570-80d38bf9dae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513979}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513837} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3f9993-5662-5d10-ad04-17586e5bdd3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd31a01e-94fe-56c0-b345-2a845f22f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138131" :question/problem "The maximum  value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum  is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f0ea0ef3-3e78-56ab-a0ba-c9335fb9c57e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410175} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410180} {:answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410183} {:answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "13f589cb-239b-530f-99ce-e58d907d8d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-136828" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7f8da2b-1589-5952-8f0d-950254eb7239" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1700f9a6-fd4f-55e6-a5b2-c7c162c8b09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410167} {:answer/id #uuid "56db98f5-8954-5a74-9db5-ea9ad36d4145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410174} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd6a7be-2123-5e1c-93b5-fa6a69ebca48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410178} {:answer/id #uuid "6f32c544-2ebd-5102-86f5-09f04cfa659e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0dcfdc-b7d8-5406-814d-16ab7d147b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410179} :answer-field/id #uuid "62b7e0f0-3a2a-5bad-a1a2-7313ccefaabb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5e6637da-fa52-5317-a60a-ab7680b44bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138128" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fcea7f08-d34b-5424-a080-6ea8ec8f6d7c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514543}}] :activity/title "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513520}]}
